      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Given that \\(\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\\), we start by expressing the logarithms in a form that allows us to equate their bases.\n\nFirst, we use the change of base formula for logarithms. The change of base formula states that \\(\\log_a b = \\frac{\\log_c b}{\\log_c a}\\). Applying this to our problem, we get:\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\frac{\\log 4}{\\log x} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log 3}{\\log 27}\n\\]\nSince \\(4 = 2^2\\) and \\(27 = 3^3\\), we can rewrite the logarithms as:\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\frac{\\log (2^2)}{\\log x} = \\frac{2 \\log 2}{\\log x}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log 3}{\\log 27} = \\frac{\\log 3}{3 \\log 3} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\nThus, the equation \\(\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\\) becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{2 \\log 2}{\\log x} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\nTo solve for \\(\\log x\\), we cross-multiply:\n\\[\n2 \\log 2 = \\frac{1}{3} \\log x \\implies 6 \\log 2 = \\log x \\implies \\log x = 6 \\log 2 = \\log 2^6 = \\log 64\n\\]\nTherefore, \\(\\log x = \\log 64\\), which implies:\n\\[\nx = 64\n\\]\nTo verify, we substitute \\(x = 64\\) back into the original equation:\n\\[\n\\log_{64} 4 = \\frac{\\log 4}{\\log 64} = \\frac{\\log (2^2)}{\\log (4^2)} = \\frac{2 \\log 2}{2 \\log 2} = 1\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log 3}{\\log 27} = \\frac{\\log 3}{3 \\log 3} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\nSince both sides are equal, our solution is correct. The value of \\(x\\) is \\(\\boxed{64}\\).",
