      "text": "We start with the given equation:\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\n\\]\n\nFirst, we need to express each logarithm in terms of the natural logarithm or a common base for simplicity. We use the change of base formula:\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\frac{\\log 4}{\\log x}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log 3}{\\log 27}\n\\]\n\nNext, we simplify \\(\\log_{27} 3\\). Recall that:\n\\[\n27 = 3^3 \\implies \\log_{27} 3 = \\log_{3^3} 3 = \\frac{\\log 3}{\\log 3^3} = \\frac{\\log 3}{3 \\log 3} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log 4}{\\log x} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nWe convert \\(\\log 4\\) to its base 2 form:\n\\[\n\\log 4 = \\log (2^2) = 2 \\log 2\n\\]\n\nSubstitute this into the equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{2 \\log 2}{\\log x} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nTo eliminate the fraction, multiply both sides by \\(\\log x\\):\n\\[\n2 \\log 2 = \\frac{1}{3} \\log x\n\\]\n\nNext, solve for \\(\\log x\\). Divide both sides by \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\):\n\\[\n\\log x = 2 \\log 2 \\times \\frac{3}{1} = 6 \\log 2\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\(\\log 2\\) is a constant, and we can use the properties of logarithms:\n\\[\n\\log x = \\log 2^6 = \\log 64\n\\]\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\\[\nx = 64\n\\]\n\nFinally, we box the final answer:\n\\[\n\\boxed{64}\n\\]",
