      "text": "Given $\\log_x 4=\\log_{27} 3$, we can rewrite the equation using the change of base formula and the property of logarithms.\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the left side:\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\frac{\\log 4}{\\log x}\n\\]\nSince $4 = 2^2$, we have $\\log 4 = \\log (2^2) = 2\\log 2$.\n\nSo, the equation becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{2\\log 2}{\\log x} = \\log_{27} 3\n\\]\n\nNext, let's rewrite the right side using the change of base formula:\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log 3}{\\log 27}\n\\]\nSince $27 = 3^3$, we have $\\log 27 = \\log (3^3) = 3\\log 3$.\n\nSo, the equation now is:\n\\[\n\\frac{2\\log 2}{\\log x} = \\frac{\\log 3}{3\\log 3}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the right side, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{2\\log 2}{\\log x} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nCross-multiplying to solve for $\\log x$, we have:\n\\[\n2\\log 2 = \\frac{\\log x}{3}\n\\]\n\\[\n6\\log 2 = \\log x\n\\]\n\\[\n\\log x = \\log (2^6)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\log x = \\log 64\n\\]\n\nSince the logarithms are equal, we can equate the arguments:\n\\[\nx = 64\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of $x$ is $\\boxed{64}$.",
